From 337cfff5ad9d5c5d44c44250664beb63c9a0d14a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:55:34 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 66/66] urltest: the loss of members over QUIC (hysteria2,
 tuic)

The loss mode counted a member's loss by the retransmissions of a TCP connection of its own to
the server. A member over QUIC has none: its connections are streams of one QUIC connection, so
its probe ended in ErrLossNotOwnConnection and the member was chosen by its delay alone.

A hysteria2 or tuic outbound now tells the counts of its QUIC connection
(adapter.QUICStatsOutbound): the server's 1-RTT packets that came and the packet numbers that did
not, counted by the receiver in quic-go (the podkop-engine fork, ConnectionStats), and the RTTs.
LossProbeQUIC downloads the loss URL in windows of probesock.Window, by its "bytes" parameter or
by ranges, and counts the probe by the packets the connection received across it. It counts only
when the connection stayed the same, the packets had the reorder wait of RFC 9002 (9/8 of the
smoothed RTT) to come, and no UDP socket of the host dropped a datagram on receive
(/proc/net/snmp, RcvbufErrors) meanwhile; a probe cut by its time limit counts as far as it came,
as the TCP one does. The probe holds a stream of its own open across the download: a member
nothing references closes its connection with its last stream, and an HTTP connection closed
between windows, or by the cut, would close it before it was counted. The connection of the
member in use carries its users' traffic too: its sample weighs as many packets as the probe took,
at the share the connection lost, or one busy probe would outweigh the window of hours. A loss URL
with neither ranges nor the parameter counts nothing (ErrLossNoWindows) and rests like a member
without a connection of its own; one whose file ends at the end of a window (416 for the next)
counts what came.

The loss weighs a member's delay at the price of its transport: of the outbound a member that is
a group selects, whose tag its loss is kept by, and whose connection its probe counts. Over QUIC a
lost packet costs its stream a round of recovery and no new connection a handshake timer: the
price fitted to the measured slowdown of hysteria2 pages (BBR, RTT 20 to 150 ms), over the cells
of 0.5 to 2 % of loss where a member is chosen over another, makes 1 % cost x1.03 at 20 ms and
x1.44 at 100 ms (measured x1.05 and x1.45), where TCP's costs x2.85 and x1.57. A fit over all the
cells, 5 and 10 % with them, priced 1 % at x1.24 at 100 ms. An RTT below the 20 ms measured is
priced as 20 ms. A slowdown below 1 weighs nothing down.

On a stand (netem on both ways, 20 probes of 1 MB a level) the count came within 0.5 points of
the loss of 0.5 to 5 %, as close as the TCP count. On a clean path a probe on a new connection
counts about 2 packets of a megabyte (0.2 %): the server's quic-go skips packet numbers to detect
optimistic acknowledgements (RFC 9000, 21.4), one in the first 256 and one in each period that
doubles after, and a skipped number reads as a lost one. A member nothing references closes its
connection with its last stream (the idle connections of unreferenced outbounds are closed), so
an unselected member is probed on a new connection and shows it; the member in use does not. Not
seen on the stand, whose path takes 1500 bytes: a server's probes of the path MTU (quic-go, up to
1452 bytes, on a new connection) that a narrower path drops count as lost too, and so do the
packets a router too slow for the server's rate drops before quic-go reads them.

